'''''Briza maxima''''' is a species of the [[Poaceae|grass]] genus ''[[Briza]]''. It is native to [[Northern Africa]], [[Western Asia]] and [[Southern Europe]] and is cultivated or naturalised in the [[British Isles]], the [[Azores]], [[Australasia]], the western [[United States]], [[Central America|Central]] and [[South America]], and [[Hawaii]].<ref name=grin>{{GRIN | access-date=2007-10-18}}</ref>

This species has a large number of common names, including '''big quaking grass''', '''great quaking grass''', '''greater quaking-grass''',<ref name=BSBI07>{{BSBI 2007 |access-date=2014-10-17}}</ref> '''large quaking grass''', '''blowfly grass''', '''rattlesnake grass''', '''greater rattlesnake grass''', '''shelly grass''', '''rattle grass''', and '''shell grass'''.<ref>[http://webarchive.loc.gov/all/20090627190948/http://www.joondalup.wa.gov.au/cms/filedisplay.asp?file=444 Joondalup Coastal Foreshore Natural Areas Management Plan] Retrieved 2007-10-15.</ref><ref>[http://www.weeds.asn.au/weeds/bot_to_comm.html ''Briza maxima''.] {{webarchive |url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070828213209/http://www.weeds.asn.au/weeds/bot_to_comm.html |date=August 28, 2007 }} National Weeds Strategy. Retrieved 2007-10-15.</ref><ref name=grin/>

It grows to a height of 60&nbsp;cm. Their spikelets resemble those of the unrelated species ''[[Bromus briziformis]].'' The seeds and leaves are edible.<ref>{{cite web|title=''Briza maxima''|url=http://www.survival.org.au/bf_briza_maxima.php|work=Survival and Self Sufficiency|access-date=9 September 2011}}</ref>

